      "writtenOn": "2026-02-06T19:34:23Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "There are three things going on that require us to check for bounds intersections in a layer:\n1. The draw does shading and it depends on the dst, so if we overlap a prior regular draw, we have to be added to a later layer.\n2. The draw is a depth-only clip draw, so if we overlap a prior regular draw we have to be added to a later layer.\n3. The draw uses the stencil buffer, so if we overlap anything that also uses the stencil, we have to be in a *different* layer (could move earlier if 1 or 2 don\u0027t force it to be a later layer). \n\nThe other overlap scenarios that don\u0027t really matter (assuming no stencil use) are:\n1. The draw does shading and we overlap a depth-only clip draw. There are two subcases here. \n\n  Firstly, if the clip draw belongs to an active clip element, then we\u0027ll have encountered this layer when visiting the clip stack so this layer must be the most recent deferred layer that will influence the draw. We already won\u0027t go earlier than that layer, and we can be put into this layer since the depth draws are drawn first.\n\n  Secondly, if the clip draw belongs to an inactive clip element, that means the clip has already updated its max Z value because it was popped off the clip stack, and this draw will have a max Z value greater than the clip\u0027s. If the draw doesn\u0027t depend on dst, we can go earlier than this layer still because its pixels won\u0027t be occluded by the clip and can occlude any of the other later draws. If we do depend on the dst, either we\u0027re blocked naturally from also intersecting some of the draws that were clipped by this clip draw, or we don\u0027t intersect any of those draws (just the clip area influencing them). That means we can still go earlier because any pixels the draw would block for this inactive clip draw won\u0027t be pixels that actually have to participate in clipping any of its influenced draws (since this draw doesn\u0027t intersect any of those draws). \n  \n2. The draw is a depth-only clip draw and overlaps a depth-only draw, which has two similar subcases:\n \n   Firstly, the other depth draw is also an active clip element. There are three subsubcases to think about:\n   \n      a. The two depth-only draws are in the same stack frame of the clip stack. This means that they are allowed to affect the same set of draws so can have the same max Z value. If the don\u0027t end up with the same max Z value, it\u0027s because the maximal set of affected draws had disparate overlaps with each element. In the area of overlap between the two elements, it doesn\u0027t matter which shape wins the depth test as either will clip the relevant shaded pixels.\n      \n      b. The other draw is shallower in the clip stack than this draw. That means all the draws affected by the other clip element can also be affected by this limit. Because we\u0027re only adding this deeper element now means none of those draws would be influenced by this clip. Any region where the two clips overlap then fall into the same scenario as a), or the other clip will be popped off the stack and a new draw will come in that increases this draw\u0027s max Z. That\u0027s okay because it\u0027ll still be clipping everything it should.\n      \n      c. The other draw is deeper in the clip stack than this draw, which is the reverse scenario as b). We can move this clip draw earlier because both clip draws can influence any regular draws that get recorded later until this draw is popped off the clip stack. Once that happens, the other clip draw could continue to influence new draws and increase its max Z value and overwrites any shared pixels.\n      \n   Secondly, if it is overlapping with an inactive clip element\u0027s depth draw, then assuming we aren\u0027t blocked from going earlier by overlapping any previous draws, we know we aren\u0027t going to damage the inactive clip\u0027s pixels that are used for clipping those previous draws (just like the above 1\u0027s secondary case).\n\n\nThese ordering behaviors are handled in the sort drawList code based on how the BoundsManager and DisjointStencilSet intersection tree\u0027s are updated.\n- The ClipStack\u0027s element walking ensures all affected draws sort after the clip draws\n- The BoundsManager query in drawGeometry() ensures the affected draws sort after other regular draws.\n- The BoundsManager query in the updateForDraw() ensures the clip element sorts after regular draws.\n- Only regular draws write into the BoundsManager.\n- drawGeometry() and drawClipShape() update the intersection tree set if the draws use stencil to keep things disjoint (but doesn\u0027t otherwise enforce an ordering on the draws).\n\nFor the layering approach, most of the layer test checks don\u0027t really care about whether or not it\u0027s a depth-only draw because the behaviors we need to enforce exactly match what we do for a regular draw that depends on dst. We just need to know whether or not to enforce painters order on overlap, and whether or not to keep disjoint for stencil. The depth-only part comes in for making sure we have that binding list walked first for a given layer.",
